\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Independent Gaussian laws on real isotypic sectors}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Let a finite group #{G} act orthogonally on a finite-dimensional
real inner-product space #{H}, and let #{Q>0} be self-adjoint and
#{G}-equivariant. Write the [orthogonal isotypic projectors](fdip-004V)
as #{E_\Xi}, put #{H_\Xi=E_\Xi H}, and omit the zero sectors.
Use [induced Euclidean volumes](fdip-002M) and uncompleted Borel
sigma-algebras. For the normalized Gaussian law #{\mu_Q}, the map
##{\mathcal D:H\longrightarrow\prod_\Xi H_\Xi,
\qquad z\longmapsto(E_\Xi z)_\Xi}
satisfies
##{\mathcal D_*\mu_Q=\bigotimes_\Xi\mu_{Q|_{H_\Xi}}.
\qquad Z_Q=\prod_\Xi Z_{Q|_{H_\Xi}}.}
Thus the projected fields are independent. Each has covariance
#{(Q|_{H_\Xi})^{-1}} on its own real sector.}

\p{In the [multiplicity coordinates](fdip-0050), set
#{D_\Xi=\operatorname{End}_G(W_\Xi)},
#{r_\Xi=\dim_{D_\Xi}W_\Xi}, and
#{d_\Xi=\dim_{\mathbb R}D_\Xi\in\{1,2,4\}}.
Write #{Q_\Xi\in M_{m_\Xi}(D_\Xi)} for the multiplicity matrix;
it is not the entire real-sector matrix. Then
##{\begin{aligned}
\det_{\mathbb R}Q
 &=\prod_\Xi\det_{\mathbb R}
   (Q_\Xi:D_\Xi^{m_\Xi}\to D_\Xi^{m_\Xi})^{r_\Xi},\\
Z_{Q|_{H_\Xi}}
 &=\frac{(2\pi)^{m_\Xi r_\Xi d_\Xi/2}}
 {\det_{\mathbb R}(Q_\Xi)^{r_\Xi/2}}.
\end{aligned}}
The determinants in these formulas are ordinary determinants of real
linear operators, including when #{D_\Xi=\mathbb H}.}

\proof{
\p{The projectors are self-adjoint, mutually orthogonal and sum to
#{I}. Addition from the product of their images is an isometry inverse
to #{\mathcal D}. Concatenated orthonormal bases identify the
[orthogonal product volume](fdip-002Q) with volume on #{H};
finite products of these Borel spaces have the product Borel
sigma-algebra. Commutation with every projector makes #{Q}
block diagonal, so its positive quadratic form is the sum of its
sector quadratic forms. The exponential weight therefore factors.}

\p{Each restricted precision is positive, and the
[Gaussian normalizer theorem](fdip-002T) makes every sector integral
finite and positive. Nonnegative Tonelli gives the product of
normalizers. Integrating the factored density on a Borel rectangle
gives the product of its sector probabilities; uniqueness of finite
measures on the rectangle-generated sigma-algebra gives the stated
product law. This is the same measure argument as in
\ref{fdip-000M}, now for the finite family of projectors.
The [inverse-precision covariance theorem](fdip-000B) applies to each
factor. Empty sectors have unit normalizer and their one-point law.}

\p{For the determinant, the real-sector matrix is the direct sum of
#{r_\Xi} identical real operators #{Q_\Xi} on
#{D_\Xi^{m_\Xi}}, by the orthonormal scalar-column construction of
\ref{fdip-0050}. Determinants multiply under real direct sums.
If the positive Hermitian eigenvalues of #{Q_\Xi} are
#{\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_{m_\Xi}}, its real determinant is
#{\prod_j\lambda_j^{d_\Xi}}. Substitution into the real Gaussian
normalizer gives the displayed formula.}
}

\p{For the [twisted prism](fdip-0052), the trivial multiplicity matrix
has determinant #{4}, while the complex matrix has complex determinant
#{46}. Both have #{r_\Xi=1}, so
#{\det_{\mathbb R}Q=4\cdot46^2=8464} and
#{Z_Q=(2\pi)^3/92}. For the
[binary tetrahedral Cayley graph](fdip-0053), write #{a=m^2>0}.
The 5 real sectors give the following factors:
##{\begin{array}{c|c|c}
D_\Xi&r_\Xi&\det_{\mathbb R}(Q|_{H_\Xi})\\\hline
\mathbb R&1&a\\
\mathbb C&1&(a+3)^2\\
\mathbb R&3&[(a+8)(a+11)^2]^3\\
\mathbb H&1&(a+9)^4\\
\mathbb C&2&[(a+6)(a+9)]^4
\end{array}}
Their product is
##{\det_{\mathbb R}Q
 =a(a+3)^2(a+6)^4(a+8)^3(a+9)^8(a+11)^6.}
The exponents sum to #{24}, the real field dimension. The normalizer is
##{Z_Q=\frac{(2\pi)^{12}}{\sqrt{\det_{\mathbb R}Q}}.}
These factors use the mass-shifted precision, not the singular
Laplacian alone.}

\p{The density convention agrees with
\citet{Theorem 8.4, p. 385}{friedli2017statistical} after setting
its mean to zero and its covariance to #{\Sigma=Q^{-1}}.
That theorem identifies the Gaussian density; the finite-group
projectors and real determinant exponents are supplied by the proofs
above and in \ref{fdip-0050}.}

\p{Mathlib's
\leanref{ProbabilityTheory.HasGaussianLaw.iIndepFun_of_covariance_inner}
supports the independence step for a finite family of jointly
Gaussian real Hilbert-space variables. Here the family is
#{z\mapsto(E_\Xi z)_\Xi}, a linear image of the Gaussian field.
For distinct sectors and #{u\in H_\Xi}, #{v\in H_\Psi}, its
scalar cross covariance is #{\langle u,Q^{-1}v\rangle=0},
because #{Q^{-1}} preserves the orthogonal sectors. Thus the
declaration's zero-cross-covariance hypothesis matches this family.
It does not assert the representation-theoretic decomposition or the
real determinant exponents.}
